1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,303
Everyone has a plan for your first €1,000.

2
00:00:03,343 --> 00:00:04,928
Most of them are wrong.

3
00:00:05,133 --> 00:00:07,035
Your bank offers a fund.

4
00:00:07,075 --> 00:00:09,112
A friend talks about crypto.

5
00:00:09,152 --> 00:00:11,660
Someone online has a secret strategy.

6
00:00:12,333 --> 00:00:13,711
So you do nothing.

7
00:00:13,751 --> 00:00:16,514
And inflation eats a little of it every year.

8
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:20,309
But your first €1,000 isn't about getting rich.

9
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:22,593
It's about building the habit that will.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:24,854
Eight steps, in order.

11
00:00:24,894 --> 00:00:26,010
One warning:

12
00:00:26,050 --> 00:00:27,506
you can lose money.

13
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,471
One. Kill expensive debt first.

14
00:00:32,067 --> 00:00:33,412
If you have a credit card

15
00:00:33,452 --> 00:00:35,617
or a loan at 15 or 20% a year,

16
00:00:35,657 --> 00:00:37,193
pay that off first.

17
00:00:37,233 --> 00:00:40,625
Every euro you pay back is 20% you stop paying.

18
00:00:40,665 --> 00:00:42,947
No investment can promise that.

19
00:00:44,133 --> 00:00:46,934
Two. Keep a small safety cushion.

20
00:00:47,467 --> 00:00:50,437
Before investing, keep some cash for surprises:

21
00:00:50,477 --> 00:00:53,163
a broken phone, a car repair, a big bill.

22
00:00:53,233 --> 00:00:55,103
Start with one month of expenses,

23
00:00:55,143 --> 00:00:57,158
and build towards three to six.

24
00:00:57,198 --> 00:00:59,296
Otherwise, the first emergency forces you

25
00:00:59,336 --> 00:01:01,096
to sell at the worst moment.

26
00:01:01,667 --> 00:01:04,226
And if €1,000 is all you have?

27
00:01:04,266 --> 00:01:05,902
Then that's your cushion.

28
00:01:05,942 --> 00:01:09,058
Keep it, and start with a small monthly plan instead.

29
00:01:10,133 --> 00:01:12,639
Three. Use the right account.

30
00:01:13,167 --> 00:01:15,403
Many European countries have accounts

31
00:01:15,443 --> 00:01:16,942
with tax advantages.

32
00:01:16,982 --> 00:01:18,811
The PEA in France.

33
00:01:18,851 --> 00:01:20,675
The ISA in the UK.

34
00:01:20,715 --> 00:01:22,582
The ISK in Sweden.

35
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:25,183
The same investment, inside the right account,

36
00:01:25,223 --> 00:01:27,938
can leave you with noticeably more after tax.

37
00:01:27,978 --> 00:01:29,959
Check what your country offers.

38
00:01:30,967 --> 00:01:34,303
Four. Pick a regulated, low-cost broker.

39
00:01:35,067 --> 00:01:36,775
Look for a broker authorised

40
00:01:36,815 --> 00:01:38,621
by a financial regulator.

41
00:01:38,661 --> 00:01:40,944
In the EU, if a broker fails and can't

42
00:01:40,984 --> 00:01:42,282
give your investments back,

43
00:01:42,322 --> 00:01:45,053
a compensation scheme steps in, usually up

44
00:01:45,093 --> 00:01:47,358
to €20,000 or more.

45
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:48,982
Then look at the fees:

46
00:01:49,022 --> 00:01:51,940
per order, per year, and for currency exchange.

47
00:01:51,980 --> 00:01:55,827
On €1,000, a €10 fee is already 1%.

48
00:01:56,800 --> 00:01:59,249
Five. Buy the whole market.

49
00:01:59,900 --> 00:02:02,685
Instead of guessing which company will win,

50
00:02:02,725 --> 00:02:05,117
buy them all, with one global index ETF.

51
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:07,363
One fund can hold well over a thousand

52
00:02:07,403 --> 00:02:09,365
companies across dozens of countries,

53
00:02:09,405 --> 00:02:12,559
for around 0.2% a year or less.

54
00:02:12,599 --> 00:02:14,856
In Europe, look for UCITS in the name.

55
00:02:15,967 --> 00:02:18,024
Six. Automate it.

56
00:02:18,733 --> 00:02:21,693
Many brokers let you set up a monthly savings plan,

57
00:02:21,733 --> 00:02:23,274
sometimes for free.

58
00:02:23,314 --> 00:02:25,353
Even €50 a month.

59
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:28,560
Your first €1,000 becomes the start of a habit,

60
00:02:28,600 --> 00:02:30,098
not a one-off bet.

61
00:02:30,667 --> 00:02:33,319
€1,000 left alone for thirty years

62
00:02:33,359 --> 00:02:37,966
at 7% a year grows to about €7,600.

63
00:02:38,006 --> 00:02:42,679
Add €100 a month, and it's about €125,000.

64
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:46,513
About €37,000 of that is money you put in.

65
00:02:46,553 --> 00:02:49,288
And that's before fees, inflation and tax.

66
00:02:50,267 --> 00:02:52,593
Seven. Then don't touch it.

67
00:02:53,133 --> 00:02:54,518
In early 2020,

68
00:02:54,558 --> 00:02:57,359
stock markets fell about a third in a few weeks.

69
00:02:57,399 --> 00:02:59,557
People who sold locked in the loss.

70
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:02,910
The S&P 500, the big US index,

71
00:03:02,950 --> 00:03:04,912
was back at its record about five months

72
00:03:04,952 --> 00:03:06,315
after the bottom.

73
00:03:06,967 --> 00:03:08,935
It isn't always that fast.

74
00:03:08,975 --> 00:03:10,052
After 2000,

75
00:03:10,092 --> 00:03:13,037
US stocks needed about seven years to get back,

76
00:03:13,077 --> 00:03:15,204
then 2008 hit.

77
00:03:15,244 --> 00:03:16,956
Thirteen years in all.

78
00:03:16,996 --> 00:03:18,451
You can lose money.

79
00:03:19,067 --> 00:03:20,816
Don't check it every day.

80
00:03:20,856 --> 00:03:22,720
Once a month is plenty.

81
00:03:22,760 --> 00:03:24,510
Once a year is better.

82
00:03:25,667 --> 00:03:28,116
Eight. Keep learning, slowly.

83
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:30,410
Read one good book.

84
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:31,818
Watch the fees.

85
00:03:31,858 --> 00:03:35,311
Ignore anyone promising fast, guaranteed returns.

86
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:38,326
If it sounds too good to be true, it usually is.

87
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:40,829
Final message.

88
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:44,040
The best first investment isn't a stock.

89
00:03:44,080 --> 00:03:45,255
It's a system:

90
00:03:45,295 --> 00:03:48,060
no expensive debt, a cushion, the right account,

91
00:03:48,100 --> 00:03:51,202
one cheap global fund, and automatic deposits.

92
00:03:51,933 --> 00:03:53,198
Boring?

93
00:03:53,238 --> 00:03:56,150
Yes. That's exactly why it works.

94
00:03:56,867 --> 00:03:59,722
This is education, not financial advice.

95
00:03:59,762 --> 00:04:02,542
Rules differ by country, so check yours.

96
00:04:02,582 --> 00:04:04,349
If this helped, subscribe.

97
00:04:04,389 --> 00:04:07,936
Next up: compound interest, explained simply.
